# arvo-standards

The industrial conventions, written as aliases over the primitives.

A consumer who already thinks in a standard's vocabulary should be able to keep thinking in it and get
arvo underneath. So `fi` and `fimath` are here as names you write, and behind each is a declared
signature from `arvo-format` with nothing hidden in between: the alias is the convention's coordinates
mapped onto the ones the format carries, and the mapping is the whole of it.

Alias is meant literally. There is no adapter, no shim, no second implementation of the arithmetic.
The convention names a width, a scaling and a rounding-and-overflow pair, arvo names a slot range, a
quantum and an adaptation, and each of the first three is one of the second three under a different
word. If a convention needed anything more than that, it would be telling you something about arvo
rather than about itself.

That is also what the crate is for beyond convenience. A convention that cannot be written this way is
not a convention arvo is bad at supporting, it is a gap in the primitives, and the alias is where that
shows up as a compile error instead of as a paragraph. Two of them are marked in the design already:
one rounding method the vocabulary has no name for, and a width range that runs a thousand times past
what a slot index carries.

The parity arms are the other half. They take what the standard's own documentation says the answer is
and assert arvo returns that, so the check is against the published spec rather than against arvo's
reading of it.

`no_std`, no allocation, sizes const.

## Status

Design phase. MATLAB `fi` and `fimath` are written, IEEE 754 is in scope and not yet started, and
SystemC `sc_fixed`, Vitis `ap_fixed` and Algorithmic C `ac_fixed` are reserved for later.
